{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# Implementation complete on worktree `lamb-push-status` — three design corrections worth recording\n\nThe approved B4 remedy is built: `src/rebar/_store/push_state.py` (a durable marker), recorded at\n`push.py`'s single `_raise_if_strict` choke point, exposed as `rebar.push_status()` and as a\n`push_status` field on every MCP write-tool result. 16 new tests, all driving a REAL local bare\norigin with a REAL declining `pre-receive` hook.\n\n**Mutation-checked, so the tests are not tautologies.** Removing the one `record_failure` line\nturns exactly the 5 behaviour tests RED (and leaves the 5 contract-guard tests green, as they\nmust be); removing the two `clear` calls turns only the marker-clears test RED. Each half of the\nfix is independently pinned.\n\n## Three corrections the drive forced — each caught by a real failure, not by review\n\n**1. The marker belongs in the tracker's GIT DIR, not its working tree.** My plan (D2) said a\ndot-file beside the events, following `.ensure-hinted`. That was wrong, and two existing held-out\ntests said so: `test_commit_and_push_tickets_branch_heldout` pins that a failed push leaves the\ntracker's `git status` BYTE-IDENTICAL. `push.py` sets a dirty working tree aside (stash-commit ->\nmerge -> restore) to reconcile a non-fast-forward, so a marker in the working tree perturbs\nexactly the state that dance operates on — and would show up as an untracked file in every store\non earth. It now lives in the git dir, resolved without a git subprocess and following a linked\nworktree's `gitdir:` pointer. That makes \"can never be committed or pushed\" structural rather\nthan merely conventional.\n\n**2. Deliberately NO `.json` suffix.** `test_push_rejection_surfacing` asserts \"no event was\nwritten\" via `tracker.rglob(\"*.json\")`, and rglob descends into the git dir. Rather than weaken a\nheld-out guard whose intent is sound, the artifact is named so nothing scanning for a ticket\nevent can miscount it.\n\n**3. `remote-not-found` / `invalid-destination` are NOT delivery failures.** Measured: with them\nrecorded, EVERY local-only store — an explicitly supported mode, which `push.py` skips on purpose\n— reported `state=pending, reason=remote-not-found` forever. That is the alarm-fatigue failure\nmode that would have made this fix worse than the defect. They join `push-disabled` and\n`async-delivery-unobservable` in `NON_FAILURE_REASONS`: the set of \"no push was ever attempted\".\n\n## One scope REDUCTION vs the plan\n\nD5 proposed minting a canonical `write_ack.schema.json`. It is not needed and was not added: the\nten `-> str` write tools become one shared `WriteAckOut {result, push_status}` — a strict superset\nof the `{result: <str>}` FastMCP already advertised — and both the MCP schema-drift gate and the\noutput-schema coverage test pass with them still classified as generic acks. A canonical schema\npins a CLI/library `--output json` shape, and these acks are MCP-only (`rebar comment` prints no\nJSON document), so minting one would have asserted a contract that does not exist. The exemption\nreasons were updated to describe the new shape truthfully.\n\n`declare_no_file_impact` keeps its documented `structured_output=False` exemption and so carries\nno field; it is the one write tool that advertises no schema at all.\n\n## Gates\n\n`make lint` green (incl. the complexity baseline — the MCP helpers are module-level, not nested,\nwhich kept `register_write_tools` at its ceiling of 29), `make typecheck` green (503 files), MCP\nschema-drift gate green, output-schema coverage green (36), `docs/mcp-reference.md` regenerated\nwith no drift, and every touched module under the 800-LOC cap (`push.py` 786 — it SHED\n`_unpushed_summary` to `push_state`, so this change made it smaller, not larger).\n\nFull unit + interfaces suite running now; Gerrit next."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786514445146054001,"uuid":"744a1727-286b-47ea-b5cd-a76e5c9dab9a"}